  #line-at(45.54pt, 429.78pt, "A. DC Voltage at the Maximum Conversion Efficiency ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.744pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(55.32pt, 441.54pt, "When the input microwave power is low, the rectifying ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 453.24pt, "efficiency is low due to the influence of the diode's turn-on ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 465.001pt, "voltage. As the power gradually increases, the conversion ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 476.701pt, "efficiency improves and eventually reaches its peak value, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 488.521pt, "achieving the optimal dc voltage. However, as the power ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 500.221pt, "continues to rise, the efficiency of the rectifier rapidly ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 511.981pt, "decreases due to the impact of the reverse breakdown voltage, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 523.681pt, "which may also lead to damage. ")
  #line-at(55.32pt, 535.501pt, "The goal of the reconfigurable strategy proposed in this letter ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 547.201pt, "is to maintain the maximum conversion efficiency of the ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 558.901pt, "rectifier; meanwhile, as the power continues to increase, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 570.661pt, "prevent the rectifier from outputting excessively high voltage, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 582.422pt, "thereby protecting the rectifying diode from reverse breakdown. ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 594.182pt, "Therefore, it is first necessary to study the dc voltage of the ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 605.883pt, "rectifier at its maximum conversion efficiency. ")
  #line-at(55.32pt, 617.643pt, "Fig. 3 is the nonlinear model of the rectifying diode [9], ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 629.343pt, "including the series resistance ")
