  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.755pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(55.32pt, 291.72pt, "The traditional circuit protection method using limiters is ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 303.48pt, "employed for comparison. We selected two limiters from ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 315.18pt, "Mini-Circuits: the ZFLM-252-1WL-S+ (limiter 1) [11] and the ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 326.94pt, "VLM-33W-2W-S+ (limiter 2) [12]. These limiters can restrict ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 338.7pt, "the microwave power at 2.45 GHz to within -2 dBm and ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 350.46pt, "10 dBm, respectively. Their insertion losses are approximately ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 362.16pt, "4 dB and 0.6 dB around the input power level of 0 dBm, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 373.92pt, "respectively.  ")
  #line-at(55.32pt, 385.68pt, "The measurement results of the rectifier without protection, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 397.44pt, "with two PIN diodes, the limiters 1 and 2, respectively, are ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 409.14pt, "shown in Fig. 7. When the output dc voltage is around 0.8 V, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 420.9pt, "the unprotected rectifier achieves its peak efficiency of 69.8% ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 432.599pt, "at 2 dBm, which is consistent with the analysis presented in ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 444.359pt, "Section ΙΙΙ. As the input power continues to increase, the dc ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 456.12pt, "voltage will reach the reverse breakdown voltage, leading to ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 467.819pt, "circuit damage. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.76pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(99.96pt, 573.3pt, "(a)                                                            (b) ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 588.06pt, "Fig. 7. Measured (a) dc voltage and (b) efficiency of the rectifier without ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 597.06pt, "protection, with two PIN diodes, the limiters 1 and 2, respectively. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.755pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(55.32pt, 619.5pt, "Limiter 1 can successfully keep the voltage within a safe ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 631.199pt, "range; however, its high insertion loss results in very low ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 643.019pt, "efficiency. Limiter 2 fails to keep the voltage within a safe ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 654.719pt, "range and also introduces insertion loss. ")
  #line-at(55.32pt, 666.479pt, "While, the proposed reconfigurable strategy hardly reduces ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 678.178pt, "the efficiency of the rectifier at low power levels. Even when ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 689.938pt, "the input power increases (up to 0 dBm), the rectification ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 701.699pt, "efficiency only decreases by less than 6%. More importantly, ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 713.459pt, "even if the input power reaches 20 dBm (a power that is almost ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 725.158pt, "impossible to receive), the rectifier can still limit the voltage ")
  #line-at(45.54pt, 736.919pt, "within a safe range. ")
  #line-at(55.32pt, 748.679pt, "In terms of circuit cost, the PIN diode is much cheaper than a ")
  #line-at(304.56pt, 93.898pt, "limiter. Therefore, this design offers advantages over ")
  #line-at(304.56pt, 105.598pt, "traditional designs in both performance and cost. ")
